# drivers/core/conf: what is part of every kernel of this port, and the chip's identity.
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_<NAME>=y in a defconfig builds a driver into the kernel; "standard" files are always built.
# tools/hakios-config.py reads this file; docs/drivers-config.md says what each word means and how it reaches XNU.

standard bsd/dev/hakios.version.c
	help    kern.hakios.version, and the version in the boot log

standard bsd/dev/hakios.platform.c
	help    what the drivers share (the locks of the PMIC arbiter, the always-on processor, RPMh and the SMMU), and
	help    kern.hakios.drivers, the list of the drivers built in

standard bsd/dev/hakios.cryptobench.c
	help    kern.hakios.crypto_bench: the kernel's SHA and AES with the ARMv8 instructions and in C, measured when read

standard bsd/dev/hakios.stubs.c
	help    what the rest of the kernel calls in a driver that is left out: nothing, and "not there"

standard iokit/Kernel/hakios.stubs.cpp iokitcpp
	help    the same, for the calls that are C++ (the display asking whether the GPU is done with a surface)

standard iokit/Kernel/hakios.physmap.cpp iokitcpp
	help    physical memory XNU does not manage, mapped write-combined through IOKit (sys/hakios.physmap.h): the S9's
	help    DMA buffers below 4 GB (the camera's, UFS's own mode; sys/hakios.exynos9810_mem.h has where each is)

driver SOCINFO
	file    bsd/dev/hakios.socinfo.c
	help    the chip's identity from the firmware's shared memory: kern.hakios.soc_* (socinfo=1). Any Qualcomm SoC
	help    that keeps SMEM item 137; where the shared memory is comes from soc_smem=
